### FEATURE_CHAT — conversations, messages, and what they become
#
# Sign in first with auth.http; the session cookie carries from there.
#
# **Every read below resolves the caller from that session.** There is no request in this file that
# names whose conversations to return, and that absence is DECISION-CHAT-PRIVACY-01 rather than an
# omission — a route taking a person identifier in order to read would be the confused-deputy path the
# decision refuses. The one identifier naming somebody else starts a conversation and reads nothing.

@host = http://localhost:8081
@conversation = 00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000
@person = 00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000
@message = 00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000
# CHAT-ASSIGN-DIRECT-01: an active template to start a run of, and a team channel to be refused by.
@template = 00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000
@channel = 00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000

### The rail — CHAT-VIEW-CONVERSATIONS-01
# Announcements pinned first, then direct conversations by most recent message. Ordered by recency and
# never by presence. The only number here is the caller's own unread count.
GET {{host}}/api/conversations

### Start a conversation — CHAT-VIEW-CONVERSATIONS-01 step 5
# Anybody may talk to anybody active, regardless of the reporting tree (CHAT_02 §3). Running this
# twice returns the same conversation both times: the unique index on the normalised participant pair
# is the guarantee, so two people opening it in the same instant still get one row.
POST {{host}}/api/conversations
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "personId": "{{person}}"
}

### Start a conversation with somebody who has left — 422 COUNTERPART_NOT_ACTIVE
# Distinct from PERSON_NOT_FOUND on purpose: an unknown identifier means you are looking at something
# stale, a deactivated one means the colleague has gone. They send you to do different things.
POST {{host}}/api/conversations
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "personId": "{{person}}"
}

### Read a conversation — CHAT-VIEW-CONVERSATION-01
# Newest first; page backwards with `before`. Reading appends nothing and notifies nobody — no receipt
# reaches the other participant.
GET {{host}}/api/conversations/{{conversation}}/messages?limit=50

### Page backwards through history
GET {{host}}/api/conversations/{{conversation}}/messages?before=42&limit=50

### A conversation you are not in — 404 CONVERSATION_NOT_FOUND
# **Byte-identical to an unknown conversation**, deliberately. Try it with a random identifier and
# compare the two responses: same status, same code, same message. If they differed, this route would
# be a way to ask which conversations exist and, over a few probes, who is talking to whom.
GET {{host}}/api/conversations/11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111/messages

### Say something — CHAT-SEND-MESSAGE-01
# One transaction: the message and its event. The event carries no body — the log records that a
# message existed, by whom and when, never what it said.
POST {{host}}/api/conversations/{{conversation}}/messages
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "body": "Trebuie să sunăm furnizorul până joi"
}

### A blank message — 422 MESSAGE_EMPTY
# Blank counts as absent, as everywhere in this product.
POST {{host}}/api/conversations/{{conversation}}/messages
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "body": "   "
}

### Mark how far I have read — CHAT-VIEW-CONVERSATION-01 step 5
# The caller's own bookkeeping. **Forward only**: send an older message identifier afterwards and the
# marker does not move back, so scrolling up through history cannot un-read a conversation.
POST {{host}}/api/conversations/{{conversation}}/read
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "throughMessageId": "{{message}}"
}

### What may be offered here — CHAT-ASSIGN-DIRECT-01
# Everything in the answer decides whether a control **renders at all**, and nothing in it enforces
# anything. `counterpartId` is absent on the announcement channel and on a team channel — a broadcast
# has no other person and a channel has many, so neither control renders.
#
# `mayAssignTask` is **TASK's** answer over its own subtree rule, called rather than copied.
# `templates` is deliberately not filtered by that rule: a Process Owner is a per-instance position
# rather than a role, so a run may be handed to somebody a task may not be. That asymmetry is the one
# thing about this response worth reading twice.
#
# An empty `templates` is an ordinary answer and means the library is empty. The dialog says so and
# offers the way to author one; it does not vanish.
GET {{host}}/api/conversations/{{conversation}}/assignment-context

### Give the other person a task — CHAT-ASSIGN-DIRECT-01, the task path
# **There is no assignee in this body, and the absence is the security property.** The assignee is the
# conversation's counterpart, resolved server-side, so there is no parameter to vary in order to reach
# somebody else — and CHAT never grows a subtree check of its own, which would be a second
# implementation of TASK's scope and the one that goes stale.
#
# Send an `assigneeId` here and it is ignored: the request record has no such component.
#
# One downstream call, and it goes first. Only once a task exists is a **work mark** appended to the
# thread — a row carrying who, which kind, and which work, and no sentence in any language. Each
# client assembles the words it shows.
POST {{host}}/api/conversations/{{conversation}}/tasks
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "title": "Sună furnizorul de gresie",
  "description": "Comanda din București a întârziat",
  "deadline": "2026-08-25T09:00:00Z",
  "priority": "NORMAL"
}

### A deadline in the past — 422 DEADLINE_IN_THE_PAST, and no mark
# TASK's own refusal, passed through with its own code and message unchanged. The interesting part is
# what is *not* here afterwards: no work mark, because the mark is appended only after the downstream
# call succeeds. A mark pointing at work that does not exist cannot occur.
POST {{host}}/api/conversations/{{conversation}}/tasks
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "title": "Prea târziu",
  "description": null,
  "deadline": "2020-01-01T09:00:00Z",
  "priority": "NORMAL"
}

### Start a process the other person steers — CHAT-ASSIGN-DIRECT-01, the run path
# The counterpart becomes the run's **Process Owner**. They steer it and assign each step as it
# becomes reachable — they are *not* assigned its work, and no step is assigned to anybody here.
#
# The run takes the template's own name. A conversation has none to lend it, and the names a caller
# would invent — *Raluca's run* — put a person's name on work in a library everybody reads.
POST {{host}}/api/conversations/{{conversation}}/runs
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "templateId": "{{template}}"
}

### Either path in a channel — 409 CONVERSATION_NOT_DIRECT
# Told apart from not-found, unlike almost every other refusal in this feature. The caller is already
# a participant, so they can see the channel and know what kind it is; hiding the reason would leak
# nothing and would only leave them re-trying something that cannot work.
POST {{host}}/api/conversations/{{channel}}/tasks
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "title": "Ceva",
  "description": null,
  "deadline": "2026-08-25T09:00:00Z",
  "priority": "NORMAL"
}

### A conversation I am not in — 404 CONVERSATION_NOT_FOUND
# **The whole guarantee, at two more routes.** Identical to the answer an unknown identifier gives —
# same status, same code, same message. Told apart, a caller could enumerate the workspace's
# conversations by trying identifiers.
GET {{host}}/api/conversations/99999999-9999-9999-9999-999999999999/assignment-context

###############################################################################
### CHAT-MANAGE-ROOMS-01 — the roster, and bringing somebody in
###############################################################################

### Who is in this room.
###
### **This is the roster, and it is not a reporting line.** The observing zone's mark strip was reading
### TASK's assignable-people list, which answers with a manager's subtree — so a client manager holding
### no reports was offered exactly one person to name as a performer: himself, and the messages he needed
### to mark for other people were unmarkable, with nothing about it looking like a failure. The people
### you can name for work you are discussing are the people in the room.
###
### A room the caller is not in answers exactly as one that does not exist.
###
### The announcement channel legitimately answers with an empty list: it has no membership rows at all,
### because it is everybody, and a stored copy of the workspace roster would go stale the moment somebody
### joined.
GET {{host}}/api/conversations/{{group}}/participants

### Bring somebody into a group — and the room is told.
###
### A membership that changed silently would mean everything said afterwards is read by somebody the
### earlier speakers did not know was listening. Announced, never gated — the same treatment a new client
### gets. Idempotent on the membership and not on the line: a second press adds nobody and announces
### nothing.
###
### Only a group. A direct is two people by definition and the pairing is a check constraint; a channel's
### membership IS the role assignment, so adding somebody there would be claiming they hold a job the org
### chart says they do not. Both answer 409.
###
### Somebody who has left the workspace answers 422 COUNTERPART_NOT_ACTIVE rather than being added
### quietly — they would appear in the roster the mark strip reads, and work marked for them would open a
### bracket whose closure right belongs to nobody who can exercise it.
POST {{host}}/api/conversations/{{group}}/participants
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "personId": "{{person}}"
}
